+/// The settled-loop early exit of core 1 (opt-in: off in a new `Soc`; the web build turns it on with `fm1_settled_loop`).
+///
+/// Core 1 has no clock and cpu0 does not run while it does, so a loop of core 1 that reads only things that cannot change and
+/// writes nothing goes round forever in the same state. When core 1 is back at a pc it was at earlier in the same call with the
+/// same core state, the same clock and the same TIMER4 poll-detector state, one round is single-stepped through the bus
+/// (`prove_round`), which sees every store, every read that is not free of side effects, every clock jump and every hardware
+/// lock. Only if that round ends in the identical state having changed nothing, with no interrupt core 1 could take, are the
+/// remaining whole rounds skipped: their instructions are counted as run and the poll detector is advanced by the reads it
+/// would have counted. Everything else runs as it always did. Nothing is remembered between calls.
+#[derive(Default)]
+pub struct Settled1 {
+    /// the switch
